onal data. Moreover, data quality improvements often yield better results than merely increasing model size or complexity. Small models with well-tuned training strategies can significantly outperform larger foundation models, especially when adjusted for specific tasks such as variant effect prediction.", "Model 1": "Cross-validation on observational data can produce high predictive performance but often fails to evaluate how well models predict **counterfactual outcomes**—the true goal in applications like estimating variant effects or intervention impacts[4]. Standard cross-validation primarily tests a model's ability to fit observed (factual) outcomes, not the unobserved, hypothetical outcomes under different interventions or treatments[4]. As a result, models that perform well in cross-validation may be poorly calibrated or even misleading for counterfactual prediction, particularly when applied to new settings, populations, or interventions where the underlying data distribution shifts dramatically[1][4]. More robust approaches benchmark models on actual counterfactual tasks and adversarial examples, revealing that \"large multitask\" or language models can fail at counterfactual reasoning, while smaller, interpretable, single-assay models often generalize better in this context[4]. Therefore, **cross-validation alone is not a valid criterion for selecting models for counterfactual inference**; models must be evaluated directly on counterfactual tasks relevant to their intended real-world applications[4].", "Model 2": "Cross-validation models that predict counterfactual outcomes need exhaustive model interpretation to truly understand the biology and assess potential data artifacts. Sequence-to-function models can learn causal relationships from observational data, but they may also align non-causal associations depending on how they are trained. It's crucial to evaluate models on their ability to predict counterfactual scenarios rather than just relying on cross-validation with observational data. Moreover, data quality improvements often yield better results than merely increasing model size or complexity. Small models with well-tuned training strategies can significantly outperform larger foundation models, especially when adjusted for specific tasks such as variant effect prediction."}, "real_rankings": {"perplexity": 1, "openai": 2}, "real_quality_scores": {"openai": 6, "perplexity": 8}, "question_difficulty": "Easy"}
